{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QHLhJNREVjMHhAn6reA3U8","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient measurement temperature dependence Murday Cotts NMR\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"link.springer.com\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00883553\"},{\"title\":\"self diffusion coefficient study of liquid lithium\",\"url\":\"https://scholar.xjtu.edu.cn/en/publications/self-diffusion-coefficient-study-of-liquid-lithium/\"},{\"title\":\"Self-diffusion coefficients of ionic liquids measured by multinuclear benchtop NMR\",\"url\":\"https://magritek.com/?p=21016\"},{\"title\":\"s00231 011 0874 9\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00231-011-0874-9\"},{\"title\":\"subject:Self diffusion coefficients\",\"url\":\"https://fitforthem.unipa.it/subject:Self-diffusion coefficients\"},{\"title\":\"link.aps.org\",\"url\":\"https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PhysRevB.83.094302\"},{\"title\":\"www.rsc.org\",\"url\":\"https://www.rsc.org/suppdata/d3/cp/d3cp04666d/d3cp04666d1.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Pulsed field gradient magnetic resonance measurements of lithium-ion diffusion\",\"url\":\"https://dspace.mit.edu/entities/publication/104d2ab7-993c-4471-ba74-3523459e31b6\"},{\"title\":\"Self-diffusion and isotopic effects in liquid lithium\",\"url\":\"https://scilit.com/publications/a87ad4db2680c466d4fc33f00ce090a5\"},{\"title\":\"next.sciencegate.app\",\"url\":\"https://next.sciencegate.app/document/10.1557/opl.2012.1517\"}]\n\nThe search turned up the original Murday and Cotts paper only as a citation, not its data or temperature fit.\n\n- **Original paper:** Reference lists give it as Murday & Cotts, \"Self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium,\" *J. Chem. Phys.* 48, 4938–4945 (1968). Murday JS, Cotts RM (1968) Self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium. J Chem Phys 48:4938–4945. I didn't reach the paper's text, so I can't confirm its exact temperature range or Arrhenius parameters.\n- **Later modeling check:** A 2012 study by Wang and Ni (*Heat and Mass Transfer*) used an EAM potential to compute lithium self-diffusion. It reports that its calculated values agree well with Murday's experimental results at atmospheric pressure, and that the simulated coefficient rises with temperature. It also fits an Arrhenius form to the simulation output.\n- **Isotope context:** Ominia's 1986 work on self-diffusion and isotope effects in liquid lithium is relevant to the Li-6/Li-7 comparison. Its abstract says the anomalous ratio between the self-diffusion coefficients of Li6 and Li7 is explained through the mass dependence of the atomic mean free path.\n- **Other NMR work:** Ailion & Slichter (1965) and Zamir, Wayne & Cotts (1964) are cited alongside Murday & Cotts in the solid-lithium NMR literature, but those papers are about solid lithium and aren't liquid-phase diffusion measurements.\n\nFor the actual numerical values, the Murday & Cotts 1968 paper itself is the primary source to check.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cdf79a2a-b9ca-4d2e-afd7-856fe2f24685","uuid":"6b93288d-e0bc-491d-999f-49c1dd227297","timestamp":"2026-11-01T07:37:53.112Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient measurement temperature dependence Murday Cotts NMR","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01Wkv6Teofdu4cs3pgW9XpaY","content":[{"title":"link.springer.com","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00883553"},{"title":"self diffusion coefficient study of liquid lithium","url":"https://scholar.xjtu.edu.cn/en/publications/self-diffusion-coefficient-study-of-liquid-lithium/"},{"title":"Self-diffusion coefficients of ionic liquids measured by multinuclear benchtop NMR","url":"https://magritek.com/?p=21016"},{"title":"s00231 011 0874 9","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00231-011-0874-9"},{"title":"subject:Self diffusion coefficients","url":"https://fitforthem.unipa.it/subject:Self-diffusion coefficients"},{"title":"link.aps.org","url":"https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PhysRevB.83.094302"},{"title":"www.rsc.org","url":"https://www.rsc.org/suppdata/d3/cp/d3cp04666d/d3cp04666d1.pdf"},{"title":"Pulsed field gradient magnetic resonance measurements of lithium-ion diffusion","url":"https://dspace.mit.edu/entities/publication/104d2ab7-993c-4471-ba74-3523459e31b6"},{"title":"Self-diffusion and isotopic effects in liquid lithium","url":"https://scilit.com/publications/a87ad4db2680c466d4fc33f00ce090a5"},{"title":"next.sciencegate.app","url":"https://next.sciencegate.app/document/10.1557/opl.2012.1517"}]},"The search turned up the original Murday and Cotts paper only as a citation, not its data or temperature fit.\n\n- **Original paper:** Reference lists give it as Murday & Cotts, \"Self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium,\" *J. Chem. Phys.* 48, 4938–4945 (1968). Murday JS, Cotts RM (1968) Self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium. J Chem Phys 48:4938–4945. I didn't reach the paper's text, so I can't confirm its exact temperature range or Arrhenius parameters.\n- **Later modeling check:** A 2012 study by Wang and Ni (*Heat and Mass Transfer*) used an EAM potential to compute lithium self-diffusion. It reports that its calculated values agree well with Murday's experimental results at atmospheric pressure, and that the simulated coefficient rises with temperature. It also fits an Arrhenius form to the simulation output.\n- **Isotope context:** Ominia's 1986 work on self-diffusion and isotope effects in liquid lithium is relevant to the Li-6/Li-7 comparison. Its abstract says the anomalous ratio between the self-diffusion coefficients of Li6 and Li7 is explained through the mass dependence of the atomic mean free path.\n- **Other NMR work:** Ailion & Slichter (1965) and Zamir, Wayne & Cotts (1964) are cited alongside Murday & Cotts in the solid-lithium NMR literature, but those papers are about solid lithium and aren't liquid-phase diffusion measurements.\n\nFor the actual numerical values, the Murday & Cotts 1968 paper itself is the primary source to check."],"durationSeconds":5.375630982999999,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01QHLhJNREVjMHhAn6reA3U8","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
